<section class="ph"><div class="ph-media"><img class="ph-img" src="/assets/img/photos/apartment-block.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/apartment-block-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/apartment-block.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="875" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap ph-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><a href="/michigan-claim-appraisal/">Services</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><span aria-current="page">Commercial, Condo &amp; HOA Appraisal</span></nav><p class="eyebrow eyebrow-light">Appraisal service</p><h1>Commercial Insurance Appraisal in Michigan for Owners and Boards</h1><p class="ph-lede">Commercial and association claims involve bigger roofs, more buildings and more complex policies. We serve as appraiser for owners and boards when the amount of loss is in dispute.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-maize" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-glass" href="/request-claim-review/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="ph-facts"><div><dt>Property types</dt><dd>Commercial, multifamily, condo, HOA</dd></div><div><dt>Frequent issues</dt><dd>Low-slope roofs, uniformity, code</dd></div><div><dt>Approach</dt><dd>Building-by-building scope</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><h2>Larger buildings, more moving parts</h2>
<p>Commercial and association claims share the same appraisal framework as homeowner policies under Michigan law, but the numbers, the documents and the people involved are different. A single hailstorm across a Kalamazoo office park or a windstorm over a Troy condominium complex can touch dozens of roofs and hundreds of windows at once. We work as appraisers for businesses, owners of multifamily buildings, condominium associations and HOAs whose disputes are over the amount of loss.</p>
<h2>Who we work with</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Businesses and building owners</strong> with retail, office, light industrial or warehouse space, including the large low-slope roofs common on manufacturing and logistics buildings.</li>
<li><strong>Multifamily owners</strong> of apartment buildings, two-flats and mixed-use properties.</li>
<li><strong>Condominium associations</strong> where the master policy covers common elements and the line between association and unit-owner responsibility matters.</li>
<li><strong>HOAs</strong> that insure clubhouses, pools, entry structures and other shared property.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Issues that drive commercial disputes</h2>
<h3>Low-slope roofs</h3>
<p>Membrane roofs such as TPO, EPDM and modified bitumen are often argued over in terms of repairability. Hail can bruise or fracture membranes and crush insulation boards without obvious surface marks. Core samples, moisture surveys and manufacturer guidance can all matter.</p>
<h3>Uniformity across buildings</h3>
<p>Associations often want consistent roofing and siding across a complex. Policies treat matching and uniform appearance differently, and condo bylaws can add another layer. We document what exists so the appraisal panel can value the repair accurately.</p>
<h3>Code and ordinance requirements</h3>
<p>Commercial work in Michigan typically requires permits, and replacement may trigger current energy code insulation levels, fire separations or accessibility changes. Ordinance or law coverage, where purchased, often has separate sub-limits.</p>
<h3>Business income and coinsurance</h3>
<p>Some commercial disputes include business income or extra expense. Coinsurance clauses can reduce payment if the building was underinsured. Appraisal can address the amount of these losses where the policy allows, though coverage questions are resolved elsewhere.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Board approval and authority</b>For condo associations and HOAs, confirm who has authority to demand appraisal and sign off on an award. Your bylaws, management agreement and board minutes may govern this, so an association attorney is worth involving early.</div>
<h2>How we approach a commercial appraisal</h2>
<p>We start with the full policy, including endorsements, schedules of locations and values, and any prior inspection reports. We then inspect the buildings and organize findings building by building and component by component, so the insurer’s appraiser and any umpire can compare like with like. For large portfolios, we can phase the review so that agreed buildings close sooner. The <a href="/mi-claim-calculators/percentage-deductible-calculator/">percentage deductible calculator</a> helps boards understand how wind or hail deductibles may apply, and <a href="/great-lakes-claim-guides/reading-your-xactimate-estimate/">reading your Xactimate estimate</a> explains the line items you’ll see.</p>
<h2>Statewide reach</h2>
<p>From industrial properties near <a href="/mi-cities-served/flint-insurance-appraiser/">Flint</a> to resort condominiums up north and office parks in Oakland County, we serve commercial and association clients across both peninsulas. We are independent appraisers, not public adjusters or attorneys, and we don’t give legal advice.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="qa"><details><summary><span>Can a condo association demand appraisal on a master policy?</span><i class="qa-plus" aria-hidden="true"></i></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>Generally yes, if the master policy contains an appraisal clause, which Michigan law requires for fire and property policies. Confirm who on the board has authority to act under your bylaws.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>Do you handle multi-building claims?</span><i class="qa-plus" aria-hidden="true"></i></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>Yes. We organize scope building by building so agreed items can be settled while disputed ones move forward to the umpire if needed.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>Can appraisal resolve a business income dispute?</span><i class="qa-plus" aria-hidden="true"></i></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>It can address the amount of a business income loss where the policy allows appraisal of that coverage. Whether the coverage applies is a separate question for the parties or their attorneys.</p></div></details></div>
